Reason:
The original code set `SVCR_SM_MASK` without setting `TIF_SME` and without allocating `sme_state`. This caused a state where `SVCR.SM` was set but `TIF_SME` was clear, leading to out-of-bounds reads when restoring streaming SVE registers (since `SMCR_ELx.LEN` may be larger than the allocated streaming vector length). Additionally, when `SVE_SIG_FLAG_SM` was clear, `SVCR.SM` was not cleared, potentially leaving the task in unexpected streaming mode.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (sm) {
    sme_alloc(current, false);
    if (!current->thread.sme_state)
        return -ENOMEM;
}
```
```
if (sm) {
    current->thread.svcr |= SVCR_SM_MASK;
    set_thread_flag(TIF_SME);
} else {
    current->thread.svcr &= ~SVCR_SM_MASK;
    set_thread_flag(TIF_SVE);
}
```
Reason:
These changes ensure that `sme_state` is allocated before copying streaming register data, preventing out-of-bounds memory reads. By setting `TIF_SME` when `SVE_SIG_FLAG_SM` is set and clearing `SVCR.SM` when the flag is clear, the task’s SME/SVE state is kept consistent (both `SVCR.SM` and `TIF_SME` are aligned). The manipulation of `svcr`, `TIF_SVE`, `TIF_SME`, and `fp_type` is moved earlier, so a fault during data copy cannot leave the task in an inconsistent state.

